WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:06.029
The Disney+ original series The Mandalorian
is a massive hit, and its fans have about

00:00:06.029 --> 00:00:08.960
a million questions about the series' breakout
star.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:13.610
We're not entirely sure what the deal with
Baby Yoda is, but here's everything we know

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:15.150
so far.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:20.499
Yoda's species is extremely mysterious: George
Lucas famously never gave it a name, wishing

00:00:20.499 --> 00:00:24.910
the wizened Jedi Master to remain an enigma
in as many ways as possible.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.570
One thing we do know, however, is that members
of this race are not asexual, as illustrated

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:32.160
by Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.700
That film gave us a few fleeting glimpses
of Yaddle, a female member of Yoda's species

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.710
who was a member of the Jedi High Council
and even assisted in the training of a young

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:41.890
Qui-Gon Jinn.

00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:46.030
While The Mandalorian doesn't go out of its
way to clue us in as to the gender of Baby

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.660
Yoda, Doctor Pershing, an employee of the
Client, reveals the truth in the series' third

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:51.660
episode.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.240
"What did you do to it?"

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.240
"I protected him!

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:55.240
I protected him!

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:56.240
If it wasn't for me, he'd already be dead!"

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.580
And the little tyke is referred to as "he"
several times in episode 4, and Omera even

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.750
calls him the Mandolorian's "boy."

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:07.960
So at least one mystery has been solved for
sure: Baby Yoda's a baby boy!

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.960
With that out of the way, let's move on to
a question that might sound silly at first:

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.479
Is "Baby Yoda" actually the Yoda we all know
and love?

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:18.659
After all, the Jedi Master was a baby once,
and it would certainly explain the little

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:22.590
guy's importance to the remnants of the Empire
and the Rebellion alike.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:27.310
Of course, giving this question a few moments'
thought produces an obvious answer: no way.

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:31.139
At least, not unless Yoda's species practices
reincarnation.

00:01:31.139 --> 00:01:35.520
The Mandalorian takes place between the events
of the original Star Wars trilogy and the

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.009
sequel films, and you may remember that Yoda
"became one with the Force" and died during

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:41.889
the events of Return of the Jedi.

00:01:41.889 --> 00:01:46.390
While we suppose it's possible that the series
could throw us a curveball with an "actual

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.720
Yoda in an infant's body" reveal, nothing
in the show's plotting thus far indicates,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.680
to be frank, that it would take a route that's
quite so lazy.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.889
No, we're pretty sure that a new character
Baby Yoda is; the reincarnation of Yoda, he

00:01:58.889 --> 00:01:59.889
is not.

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:04.289
Of course, there is another possibility: that
Baby Yoda could be a clone of the late Jedi

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:05.289
Master.

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:09.539
It's been pointed out by astute fans that
in a promotional image featuring Doctor Pershing,

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:14.390
he appears to be wearing a patch with an insignia
that is very, very similar to those worn by

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:19.319
the clones of Jango Fett produced on the planet
Kamino, as seen in Attack of the Clones.

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:24.290
This could be a coincidence, it could be misdirection,
or, it could be a dead giveaway.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.570
Of course, if the little dude isn't Yoda,
this raises the question: what is his name?

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:33.569
Yes, we can all be pretty sure that the internet
would have initially named him "Baby Yoda"

00:02:33.569 --> 00:02:37.590
in any event, but it only continues to do
so because we haven't been clued in to what

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.560
his real name is...have we?

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.470
The answer: no, we have not.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.739
According to Dave Filoni, who directed the
first and fifth episodes of The Mandalorian's

00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:48.010
first season, this is by design.

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.260
Speaking with Entertainment Tonight, Filoni
said,

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.430
"We still want to keep a lot of things a mystery,
we don't want to go around answering things

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:55.920
and making them less special."

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.890
Told that people were simply referring to
the tot as "Baby Yoda" for lack of anything

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:02.920
else to call him, Filoni replied, quote, "That's
fine!

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.620
All part of the mystery."

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:08.560
Well, that clears up nothing, but since we
haven't even gotten official confirmation

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:12.959
of the Mandalorian's name yet, we suppose
that shouldn't be too surprising.

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:17.840
One of the most dramatic moments in The Mandalorian
so far came in the second episode, when "Mando"

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.890
is nearly killed by a vicious Mudhorn.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:24.270
Wielding nothing but a knife, the Mandalorian
lowers his head as the beast charges, but

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:29.329
the charge is interrupted by Baby Yoda, who
uses the Force to levitate the snarling monster,

00:03:29.329 --> 00:03:31.510
allowing the Mandalorian to finish it off.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.549
So, how the heck is an infant able to use
the Force?

00:03:34.549 --> 00:03:36.849
Doesn't that require super-intensive training?

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:38.700
Well...yes and no.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.769
Don't forget that, as revealed by The Phantom
Menace, children can indeed be Force-sensitive,

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:46.780
and tap into its power, with little-to-no
training.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:51.159
Consider Anakin Skywalker, whose status as
one of the best pod racers in the galaxy as

00:03:51.159 --> 00:03:55.709
a mere youngling was thanks to his insane
midichlorian count, "even higher than Master

00:03:55.709 --> 00:03:58.000
Yoda," as we recall Obi-Wan Kenobi saying.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:02.549
Sure, Anakin wasn't levitating stuff as a
child, but we find it highly likely that members

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:07.290
of Yoda's species are naturally Force-sensitive,
taking into account that the only two to be

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:11.950
introduced prior to Baby Yoda's arrival were
both esteemed Jedi Masters.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.680
Until The Mandalorian gives us a better explanation
for the little guy's Force abilities, anyway,

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.090
that's the best one we've got.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:22.360
With the release of Star Wars Episode IX:
The Rise of Skywalker swiftly approaching,

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.880
fans have obviously taken to wondering if
the cute and cuddly character's introduction

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.830
in The Mandalorian is setting us up for an
appearance in the Skywalker Saga's conclusion.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.919
Since the series takes place some time before
the events of the sequel trilogy, it could

00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:40.400
be that by the time of the events of Rise
of Skywalker, Baby Yoda would be...well, we're

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.400
not sure.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:42.400
Kid Yoda?

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.590
Tween Yoda, perhaps?

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.110
Old enough, at any rate, to potentially play
a key part in the story.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:49.900
However, we find this to be highly unlikely.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.930
There's no evidence that The Mandalorian's
creator and lead writer, Jon Favreau, has

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:59.330
coordinated in any way, shape, or form with
Skywalker writer/director J.J. Abrams, and

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:04.430
a Young Yoda cameo, while likely a crowd-pleaser,
would be off-the-charts distracting, not to

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.810
mention shoehorned in, considering what we
know about the plot of Skywalker.

00:05:07.810 --> 00:05:12.430
No, we're thinking that Baby Yoda will be
a character specific to The Mandalorian, and

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.169
that's just fine by us.

